AU  - Bhattacharyya, Sanjib
AU  - Kumar, Deepak
T2  - Naunyn-Schmiedeberg's archives of pharmacology
AB  - The rhizomes of ginger have been in use in many forms of traditional and alternative medicines. Besides being employed as condiment and flavoring agent,  it is used in the treatment of nausea, osteoarthritis, muscle pain, menstrual  pain, chronic indigestion, Alzheimer's disease, and cancer. Ginger rhizome  contains volatile oils, phenolic compounds and resins, and characterization  studies showed that [6]-gingerol, [6]-shogaol, and [6]-paradol are reported to be  the pharmacologically active components. Gingerol is a major chemical constituent  found as volatile oil in the rhizomes of ginger. It has several medicinal  benefits and used for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis, nausea, cancer, and  diabetes. Many studies have been carried out in various parts of the world to  isolate and standardize gingerol for their use as a complementary medicine. The  present review summarizes wide range of research studies on gingerol and its  pharmacological roles in various metabolic diseases.
